<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Udhampur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Udhampur is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> division of Jammu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the seat of Udhampur district, on the road from Jammu to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a>. It takes its name from Raja Udham Singh, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a> of the Dogra dynasty. The city is the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>'s Northern Command and the starting point of the railway line to Srinagar and Baramulla.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/udhampur.jpg" alt="Udhampur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Akhil2 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KrimchiTemples.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Udhampur is a city and municipal council in the Indian union territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, in the disputed Kashmir region, and the headquarters of Udhampur district. The city lies in the Shivalik range of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, at an elevation of 756 metres, in a relatively flat part of a district that is otherwise mostly mountainous and extends into the Lesser Himalayan range. Its climate is subtropical. Summer temperatures may exceed 40 degrees, and freezing temperatures have been recorded in winter. Annual rainfall is 130 cm, mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> and winter. Snow falls rarely in the centre of the city but more often in the upper reaches of the district, such as Patnitop and Sudhmahadev.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city takes its name from Raja Udham Singh and serves as the district capital and as the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>'s Northern Command. A Forward Base Support Unit of the Indian Air Force is stationed there, and Udhampur Air Force Station is closed to civilians. The Armed Forces use the city as a transit point between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a> on the road now numbered National Highway 44, the only national highway that connects Srinagar to the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. A municipal committee with 22 wards governs the town, and the district contains four sub-divisions, eight tehsils and 357 villages. Tourist attractions in the district include Patnitop, Sudhmahadev and the Chenani Nashri Tunnel.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2011 the Udhampur urban area had 91,366 inhabitants, of whom 35,507 lived within the municipal council area. Men made up 60.2 per cent of the population, a high share attributed to the large part of the population made up by the army. The literacy rate averaged 91.5 per cent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> was the dominant religion. The city has a government medical college and degree colleges. A railway link to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> opened in 2005 with the Uttar Sampark Kranti Express from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>, and the line was being extended toward Katra and on to Banihal and Baramulla.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Udhampur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
